592186045417] [17592186311848 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "866fd62c-6cd1-507c-bd3b-f0518622e71f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311848 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y={e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14e95436-1992-5d26-a51a-862b1ae1c205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311857 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{6x})}^{′}-6(u{e}^{6x}) & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x}+6u{e}^{6x}-6u{e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{6x}}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{6x}} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{6x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397006 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a01f9794-773d-5e12-a888-6dc684788a68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397006 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, sin